{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1785169736,"nonce":"NebZpUA+Wml7p1ENuY4eNlz8V9U=","target":"6f6ed6e39a8787abc8b9fb4de3bfbbb2030b8032f24bf6a74c755968ef816f6a","message":"Root-cause analysis (corrected 2026-07-27; supersedes the 2026-07-21 subject-query analysis, which is refuted — see below).\n\n\nDistinct same-author series are reported \"already tracked as \u003cother series\u003e\"\nand folded into the wrong change-id. Reproduced end-to-end with Mark's exact\nmessage-ids (evidence below).\n\n\n`LoreSeries.fingerprint` (`src/b4/__init__.py:659-665`) hashes only\n`fromemail : revision : \u003cgit_patch_id per patch\u003e`. The subject is not part of\nit. `git_patch_id` (`src/b4/__init__.py:1842-1845`) is None whenever\n`git patch-id --stable` emits nothing — which it does for any patch that has no\n`diff --git` header (plain `diff -u`/context diffs, as rdunlap sends).\n\nWhen every patch yields None, the loop at `:662-664` appends nothing and the\nfingerprint degenerates to `sha256(f\"{fromemail}:{revision}\")`. Every rev-1\nseries from one author then shares a single fingerprint.\n\n`find_existing_change_id` (`src/b4/review/tracking.py:1097-1104`) probes\n`series.fingerprint` first and returns the first hit, so a freshly tracked\nrev-1 rdunlap series matches whatever rev-1 rdunlap series was stored earliest.\n\n\nLoaded the three threads with b4's own `LoreMailbox` and computed fingerprints:\n\n- sof v1 (20260713175510) fingerprint **==** regmap v1 (20260723181215)\n  fingerprint **==** `sha256(\"rdunlap@infradead.org:1\")` **==** `1a01f1f901cc…`\n  — two entirely unrelated series, identical hash.\n- asoc v2 (20260715000525) **==** `sha256(\"rdunlap@infradead.org:2\")` **==**\n  `7c94a1912927…`.\n- Both match the change-ids in Mark's terminal output (#0): the wrongly-matched\n  target `…-1a01f1f901cc` and the generated `…-7c94a1912927`. The asoc\n  *discovered* v1 is rev-1, so it too hashes to `1a01f1f901cc` and collides with\n  the stored sof series — exactly the reported \"already tracked as\" behavior.\n- Every patch across all three threads: `has_diff=True` but\n  `git_patch_id=None` (no `diff --git`; `git patch-id --stable` outputs nothing).\n\n\nThe 2026-07-21 analysis blamed the subject+from fuzzy fallback query\n(`src/b4/mbox.py:757-761`) for pulling in a different same-author series.\nThat is wrong. lore's `s:\"…\"` is a phrase/position match, verified against the\nlive index (scrambled word order and non-adjacent words both return zero hits;\nthe tracked subject as a phrase does not match the phantom series). For both of\nMark's cases the fuzzy query returns only the correct series' own revisions and\ncannot pull the phantom series in. The fuzzy path only *surfaces* the bug by\ndiscovering a rev-1 sibling and handing it to the collapsing fingerprint; it is\nnot the cause. (Full comment history retained by git-bug.)\n\n\n`2235355` (discovered sibling re-tracked as duplicate) is the same fingerprint\nfragility with the opposite symptom — a false-negative match where a re-derived\nfingerprint fails to line up. `47d5a4c` is adjacent. Manual revision linking /\n`absorb_series_as_revision` is the recovery route today. No fix proposed here.","files":null}]}